---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import RuntimeLayout from "../../components/RuntimeLayout.astro";
import { watershedCodeTheme } from "../../styles/code-theme";

const cfg = `import watershed/presence

// One codec, one mode. \`Auto\` takes server presence where the server
// offers it and falls back to the ripple heartbeat where it does not.
let config =
  presence.config(encode_cursor, cursor_decoder())
  |> presence.with_mode(presence.Auto)`;
---

<RuntimeLayout
  slug="presence"
  description="Watershed's ephemeral tier: ripples are document-scoped, non-sequenced broadcasts that never touch converged state, and presence turns them into a live roster of who is here, backed by the server's own connection tracking where the server offers it, and by a heartbeat and TTL where it does not."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Not everything belongs in the document</h2>
    <p>
      Some signals have short validity. Examples include the station that a
      surveyor views, a cursor position, a request for attention, or a reaction.
      Do not put these signals in a channel. A channel sequences, summarizes,
      and replays data to later clients.
    </p>
    <p>
      watershed sends this traffic through the same connection but does not use
      convergence processing for it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Ripples: broadcast and forget</h2>
    <p>
      A <strong>ripple</strong> is a document-scoped, non-sequenced broadcast
      delivered to every <em>other</em> connected client and then gone. No
      ordering, no storage, no convergence, deliberately.
      <code>submit_ripple</code> sends a type tag plus a JSON payload and returns
      nothing, because there is no state to change and no ack to await;
      <code>subscribe_ripples</code> hands each inbound ripple to your handler
      with the sender's id attached. The{" "}
      <a href="/guide/ripples">build guide's ripples step</a> walks through the
      send and receive code end to end.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because a summary never contains a ripple, a client that{" "}
      <a href="/runtime/reconnect">reconnects</a> or joins late will not see the
      ripples that it missed. This behavior is suitable for a cursor but not for
      a durable reading.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Presence: who is here</h2>
    <p>
      Ripples do not report when a peer closes a tab or loses its connection.
      The peer’s last marker can remain on screen. <strong>Presence</strong>
      removes stale markers through one of two modes.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={cfg} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      A <strong>session</strong> is one tab, device, or process; a
      <strong>key</strong> groups the sessions of one authenticated person. Two
      tabs from the same user are two entries sharing a key: the roster is keyed
      by session, so they never overwrite each other. Your handler receives a
      <code>State</code> that replaces the roster wholesale, or a
      <code>Changed</code> carrying both the delta and the resulting roster; both
      hand you a complete list, so render from whichever suits.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Two implementations, deliberately different</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Server presence</strong> tracks each connection. A later client
      receives the complete roster in one snapshot. When a socket closes, the
      server removes its entry. This mode does not use a heartbeat because the
      connection is the liveness signal.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Ripple presence</strong> supports servers without presence
      tracking. Each client sends its metadata on a timer and removes peers after
      a timeout. By default, the heartbeat interval is two seconds and the
      timeout is 6.5 seconds. A later client must wait for each peer’s next
      heartbeat. Browser timer throttling can cause a temporary false timeout.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>Auto</code> chooses between them from what the server advertises in
      its handshake. <code>Ripple</code> forces the heartbeat.
      <code>Server</code> refuses to fall back and reports
      <code>UnsupportedPresence</code> instead: a silent downgrade would make
      presence look intermittently broken with nothing to point at.
      <code>presence_js.mode</code> reports which one is running, because the two
      have genuinely different failure timing and hiding that makes it
      undebuggable.
    </p>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>Send the right traffic down each tier.</strong> Presence carries
        membership and low-frequency status: which panel, which cell, typing or
        not. High-frequency cursor motion still belongs on raw ripples: presence
        exists to answer "who is here", not to be a pipe.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">In short</span>
    <p>
      Ripples are throwaway broadcasts that never touch converged state; presence
      turns them into a roster of who is here, backed by the server's own
      connection tracking where that exists, and by a heartbeat and TTL where it
      does not, behind one API that reports which it chose.
    </p>
  </div>
</RuntimeLayout>
